---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-restless-legs-syndrome`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Restless Legs Syndrome',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Restless legs syndrome', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'G25.81', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-08-14',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Restless Legs Syndrome', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Restless Legs Syndrome: Is There a Link?"
  description="Some small studies tie low vitamin D to worse restless legs syndrome, but iron is the far bigger player. What the evidence supports and what to check first."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-restless-legs-syndrome", label: "Vitamin D & RLS" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Restless Legs Syndrome</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Restless legs syndrome (RLS) is an irresistible urge to move the legs, usually with an unpleasant
      crawling sensation, that comes on at rest in the evening and eases with movement. It is a
      dopamine- and iron-related disorder of the nervous system — which is worth stating up front,
      because the most important deficiency to check in RLS is not vitamin D.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">The vitamin D evidence</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      A handful of small studies have linked low 25(OH)D to RLS. Case-control work has reported lower
      vitamin D in RLS patients than controls, and a small open-label study (Wali 2015) found that
      correcting deficiency reduced symptom-severity scores in patients who were deficient. Because
      dopaminergic pathways in the brain express vitamin D receptors, a mechanism is at least
      plausible. But the studies are small, mostly uncontrolled, and prone to the same reverse-causation
      and publication-bias problems seen across the vitamin D literature. There is no large randomised
      trial establishing that supplementation reliably treats RLS.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Iron is the priority</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The best-established modifiable factor in RLS is <strong>iron</strong>, specifically brain iron,
      reflected imperfectly by serum ferritin. Guidelines recommend checking ferritin and transferrin
      saturation in everyone with significant RLS, and treating with iron when ferritin is low
      (commonly a threshold around 50–75 µg/L, higher than the usual "normal" cut-off). Iron repletion
      has far stronger evidence than vitamin D. See our <a href="/iron" class="text-amber-700 underline">iron page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A sensible approach</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Get the basics checked</strong> — ferritin and iron studies first, plus kidney function; RLS is common in pregnancy and kidney disease.</li>
      <li><strong>Review triggers</strong> — some antihistamines, antidepressants, and antipsychotics worsen RLS, as can caffeine and alcohol late in the day.</li>
      <li><strong>Correct a genuine vitamin D deficiency</strong> if found — it is reasonable and low-risk, and may help a subset, but treat it as a bonus rather than the main strategy.</li>
      <li><strong>See a clinician for persistent symptoms</strong> — effective prescription treatments exist when lifestyle and iron measures are not enough.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Bottom line</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The vitamin D–RLS link is a genuine but weak signal. If you are deficient, correcting it is
      worthwhile and may ease symptoms in some people, but it should not distract from checking iron,
      which is where the real leverage is.
    </p>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> New or worsening restless legs deserves iron studies and a
      medication review. Do not self-supplement iron without testing — iron overload is harmful.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-restless-legs-syndrome" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
